{% assign b_full = all_products['endless-bubble-wrap-dark-blue'] %}
{% assign b_2go = all_products['endless-bubble-2go-blind-bag'] %}
{% assign bubble_collection_url = routes.collections_url | append: '/endless-bubble' %}
{%- comment -%}
  Order is the order the dots are drawn in, and each name is both the
  modifier that colours the dot and the asset that photographs it, so a
  colour is added or reordered here and nowhere else.
{%- endcomment -%}
{% assign bubble_full_colours = 'red,pink,sky,blue,green,violet' | split: ',' %}
<div class="wt-bubble__cards">
  <article class="wt-bubble__card wt-bubble__card--full">
    {%- if b_full.featured_image -%}
      {%- assign full_default = b_full.featured_image | image_url: width: 900 -%}
    {%- endif -%}
    <div class="wt-bubble__media">{% if b_full.featured_image %}<img src="{{ full_default }}" width="{{ b_full.featured_image.width }}" height="{{ b_full.featured_image.height }}" alt="{{ b_full.title }}" class="wt-bubble__img" data-wt-photo-default="{{ full_default }}">{% endif %}</div>
    <div class="wt-bubble__body">
      <div class="wt-bubble__row">
        <h3 class="wt-bubble__name">Full size</h3>
        <span class="wt-bubble__price">{{ b_full.price | money_without_trailing_zeros | default: '$20' }}</span>
      </div>
      <p class="wt-bubble__copy">Six colors, silicone, dishwasher-safe. The one that lives on the coffee table.</p>
      {%- comment -%}
        Hovering a dot shows that colour in the card photo above, the
        same gesture the 2Go row below answers to.

        The row is labelled and the dots are not, for the same reason as
        the 2Go row: six unnamed empty spans would be six unnamed
        announcements, and the label says in one line what they say six
        times. Unlike the 2Go colours these do have names, so the label
        can name them -- which is as far as this goes. They are not
        buttons: each colour is its own product, and a control that
        changes a picture but does not take you to the product it names
        would be a worse promise than the plain dot it replaced.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <div
        class="wt-bubble__swatches"
        role="img"
        aria-label="Six colors: red, pink, sky, blue, green, violet"
        data-wt-swatches
      >
        {%- for colour in bubble_full_colours -%}
        <span
          class="wt-bubble__swatch wt-bubble__swatch--{{ colour }}"
          data-wt-photo="{{ 'wt-bubble-full-' | append: colour | append: '.png' | asset_url }}"
        ></span>
        {%- endfor -%}
      </div>
      <a href="{{ routes.collections_url }}/endless-bubble" class="wt-bubble__cta">Shop full size</a>
    </div>
  </article>
  <article class="wt-bubble__card wt-bubble__card--2go">
    {%- comment -%}
      One <img> whose src the swatch row swaps, not eighteen stacked and
      cross-faded the way the hero does it. At this count the hero's
      pattern would pull every colour down before the card is even
      scrolled to; here nothing but the default is fetched until someone
      reaches for a swatch, and the row warms the rest on first contact.

      data-wt-photo-default is what the row restores to on the way out,
      so the JS never has to know whether that default came from the
      product photo or the theme setting.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- if b_2go.featured_image -%}
      {%- assign twogo_default = b_2go.featured_image | image_url: width: 900 -%}
    {%- endif -%}
    <div class="wt-bubble__media">{% if b_2go.featured_image %}<img src="{{ twogo_default }}" width="{{ b_2go.featured_image.width }}" height="{{ b_2go.featured_image.height }}" alt="{{ b_2go.title }}" class="wt-bubble__img" data-wt-photo-default="{{ twogo_default }}">{% else %}{% render 'wt-img', img: section.settings.twogo_image, label: '2Go blind bag photo', fit: 'contain' %}{% endif %}</div>
    <div class="wt-bubble__body">
      <div class="wt-bubble__row">
        <h3 class="wt-bubble__name">2Go blind bag</h3>
        <span class="wt-bubble__price">{{ b_2go.price | money_without_trailing_zeros | default: '$13.50' }}</span>
      </div>
      <p class="wt-bubble__copy">Keychain size with a carabiner. Blind bag &mdash; the color is the surprise. There are 12 new colors to collect, but 2 of them are glowing secrets! Opening a new blind bag is like a treasure hunt. Will you be the lucky one to find a glow-in-the-dark mystery bubble wrap?</p>
      {%- comment -%}
        The swatches are decorative: each carries alt="" and the row as a
        whole is labelled instead, so a screen reader hears the count once
        rather than eighteen unnamed images. role="img" is what makes the
        label stick -- aria-label on a bare div is not reliably exposed.

        Hovering one shows that colour's bag in the card photo above.
        They stay decorative images rather than becoming eighteen
        buttons: the bag is a blind bag, so no colour is a thing you can
        pick or buy, and the swap adds nothing a screen reader could act
        on. Anyone not using a pointer keeps the default photo, which is
        the same thing the row said before it could be hovered.

        wt-2go-bag-NN is the same colour as wt-2go-NN by construction --
        the pairs were matched off the packaging, and NN is the only
        thing tying them together, so the two sets have to be renamed
        together. Their source files, in order 01..18:
        1116 1119 1125 1123 1129 1132 1134 1135 1127
        1120 1121 1130 1117 1133 1131 1118 1124 1122
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <div
        class="wt-bubble__swatches wt-bubble__swatches--images"
        role="img"
        aria-label="18 colors"
        data-wt-swatches
      >
        {%- for i in (1..18) -%}
          {%- assign n = i | prepend: '0' | slice: -2, 2 -%}
          <img
            src="{{ 'wt-2go-' | append: n | append: '.png' | asset_url }}"
            width="96"
            height="96"
            alt=""
            loading="lazy"
            class="wt-bubble__swatch"
            data-wt-photo="{{ 'wt-2go-bag-' | append: n | append: '.png' | asset_url }}"
          >
        {%- endfor -%}
      </div>
      <a href="{{ b_2go.url | default: bubble_collection_url }}" class="wt-bubble__cta">Shop 2Go</a>
    </div>
  </article>
</div>
